#* {{quote-book|en|author=Thomas Browne|authorlink=Thomas Browne|chapter=[Domestic Correspondence.] Dr. Browne to His Son Thomas.—Norwich, Nov. 1, [1661.]|editor={{w|Simon Wilkin}}|title=The Works of Sir Thomas Browne|series=Bohn’s Antiquarian Library|location=London|publisher=[[w:Henry George Bohn|Henry G[eorge] Bohn]]|date=1 November 1661|year_published=1852|volume=III|page=395|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=4No0AAAAMAAJ&pg=PA395|oclc=421817|passage=Read books which are in french and Latin, for so you may retain and increase your knowledge in Latin: some times draw and '''limn''' and practise perspective.}}
#* {{RQ:Rossetti Ballads|title=Five English Poets. II. {{w|William Blake}}. (To Frederick Shields, on His Sketch of Blake’s Work-room and Death-room, 3, Fountain Court, Strand.)|stanza=4|page=314|lines=9–10|passage=This cupboard, Holy of Holies, held the cloud / Of his soul writ and '''limned'''; [...]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Herbert A[llen] Giles|authorlink=Herbert Giles|chapter=Childbirth, Childhood, and the Position of Woman|title=Adversaria Sinica|location=Shanghai|publisher=[[w:Kelly & Walsh|Messrs. Kelly & Walsh, Ltd.]]|year=1905|volume_plain=number 1|page=377|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ovNOXaBsXZAC&pg=PA377|oclc=829421550|passage=[S]he laughs—in golden tones; she sleeps—like a fragrant lily; she dresses—'''limning''' her eyebrows like those of the silkworm moth.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author={{w|Kōbō Abe}} [pseudonym; Kimifusa Abe]|chapter=30|translator={{w|E. Dale Saunders}}|title=[[w:The Woman in the Dunes|The Woman in the Dunes: Translated from the Japanese]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Alfred A. Knopf}}|year=1964|oclc=325734|edition2=1st Vintage International|location2=New York, N.Y.|publisher2=Vintage International, {{w|Vintage Books}}|year2=April 1991|pages2=226–227|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=Ife2d7K-SbQC&pg=PA226|isbn2=978-0-679-73378-2|passage=As he looked up at the rim of the hole, faintly '''limned''' in the moonlight, he mused that this searching feeling of his was perhaps jealousy.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Michiko Kakutani|authorlink=Michiko Kakutani|title=Earthlings may endanger your peaceful rationality [review of ''Mr. Spaceman'' (2000) by Robert Olen Butler]|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2000/03/10/books/books-of-the-times-earthlings-may-endanger-your-peaceful-rationality.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20150527133109/https://www.nytimes.com/2000/03/10/books/books-of-the-times-earthlings-may-endanger-your-peaceful-rationality.html|archivedate=27 May 2015|newspaper={{w|The New York Times}}|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|The New York Times Company}}|date=10 March 2000|issn=0362-4331|oclc=971436363|passage=In telling these people's stories Mr. [[w:Robert Olen Butler|[Robert Olen] Butler]] draws upon the same gifts of empathy and insight, the same ability to '''limn''' an entire life in a couple of pages, [...]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Karen Hawkins|authorlink=Karen Hawkins (author)|chapter=2|title=The Prince who Loved Me|edition=1st Pocket Books paperback|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Pocket Books}}|month=October|year=2014|page=9|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ndCKBAAAQBAJ&pg=PA9|isbn=978-1-4516-8524-4|passage=And in her mind's eye, Roland had been ''exactly'' such a man as this—tall, dark, foreboding even, with a strong jaw that bespoke a character worth knowing, and intelligence agleam in his eyes. As if to reaffirm her imagination, the sun broke through the trees to '''limn''' his broad shoulders with gold.}}
