#* {{quote-book|en|author=John Strype|authorlink=John Strype|chapter=XXV|title=Ecclesiastical Memorials; Relating Chiefly to Religion, and the Reformation of It, and the Emergencies of the Church of England, under [[w:Henry VIII of England|King Henry VIII.]] [[w:Edward VI of England|King Edward VI.]] and [[w:Mary I of England|Queen Mary the First]]. {{...|All which being New, and such as have hitherto Escaped our Writers and Historians, will Communicate much more Light to those Great Transactions in this Kingdom: And moreover Discover further the Inclinations and Influences of the Respective Princes; the Embassies and Correspondencies with Foreign Potentates and States, chiefly with respect to Religion: The Oppositions Made to It; the Troubles and Persecutions of the Professors of It: The Tempers, Practices and Events of the Two Cardinals, [Thomas] Wolsey and [Reginald] Pole, and Other Prelates and Great Men of both Parties, in the respective Reigns: Besides, Accounts of Convocations, Royal and Episcopal Visitations, Ecclesiastical Constitutions, Books from Time to Time set forth; with various other Matters Worthy of Note and Observation.}} In Three Volumes.{{nb...|With a Large Appendix to Each Volume, Containing Original Papers, Records, &c.}}|location=London|publisher=Printed for John Wyat,{{nb...|at the Rose in St. Pauls Church-yard.}}|year=1721|volume=I|section=book I|page=182|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/ecclesiastical01stry/page/n211/mode/1up|oclc=777964090|passage=Some of her &#91;{{w|Elizabeth Barton}}'s&#93; Revelations were no better than ſilly Tales: Such was a certain Tale of ''[[w:Mary Magdalene|Mary Magdalen]]'', delivering her a Letter from Heaven, that was '''limned''' with golden Letters: which indeed was written by a Monk of St. ''Auguſtines'', ''Canterbury'': and another at ''Calais''.}}

